INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: **Synthesis.** Three starting insights guide the proposal. (1) SASRec already represents sequential context. A small recent-history residual can strengthen current interests without replacing this backbone. (2) Users report both loosely related recommendations and excessive influence from old, occasional interests. Recent, repeated interactions offer observable evidence for addressing both failures, although they cannot establish unmet need. (3) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. These training-position measurements motivate better discrimination but do not establish validation gains.

Three literature insights inform implementation. (1) [MSMT-LSI](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44196-024-00460-5) separates short- and long-term preferences. This motivates a small recent branch at the current 0% research stage. (2) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939) motivates investigating negative count and loss calibration. Such changes should remain separate ablations so architectural effects remain identifiable. (3) [Krichene and Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) show limitations of sampled evaluation. Selection must therefore use the exact pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Framework.** Three directions separate intent representation, discrimination, and collaborative evidence:

| Direction | Minimal candidate | Diagnostic target |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal intent | Gated recent-history residual | Old interests outweigh current evidence |
| Learning objective | Uniform multi-negative gBCE | Weak lower-tail separation |
| Behavioral relations | Shrunk directed-transition residual | Broad affinity lacks specific behavioral support |

**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each entry gives a positive and negative assessment for every dimension.

- **Recent residual: 4/7/8/6/3.** Originality: targeted adaptation, familiar fusion. Potential: extensible intent mechanism, uncertain incremental benefit. User: addresses loose relevance and stale interests, cannot verify compatibility. Model: can improve difficult contexts, does not directly optimize margins. Difficulty: small module, prefix masking needs care.
- **Multi-negative gBCE: 3/7/4/9/4.** Originality: benchmark adaptation, established method. Potential: reusable objective, calibration needs tuning. User: improves discrimination, lacks explicit current-intent modeling. Model: directly targets separation, false negatives remain. Difficulty: compact loss change, sampling increases cost.
- **Transition residual: 4/6/7/6/5.** Originality: support-aware fusion, familiar graph signals. Potential: useful sparse evidence, weak edges limit coverage. User: favors observed connections, transitions cannot prove usable fit. Model: supplies additional evidence, may reinforce popularity. Difficulty: sparse implementation, training/inference consistency needs work.

**Selected idea: gated recent-history residual.** At 0% progress, prioritize one feasible architectural change. Fuse SASRec features with an exponentially weighted recent-item vector through a bounded scalar gate. Repeated recent items naturally accumulate weight. This explicitly targets loose historical affinity and old occasional interests; improved Electronics separation is a testable hypothesis.

```python
h = sasrec(prefix)
r = normalized_recency_pool(item_embeddings(prefix[-k:]))
g = g_max * sigmoid(gate(concat(h, r)))
z = layer_norm(h + g * r)
scores = z @ candidate_embeddings.T
```

Use causal prefixes during training and identical scoring in the predictor writer. Preserve baseline loss initially; compare baseline, fixed-gate, and learned-gate variants. Retain all four routes, seeds, splits, artifact paths, and evaluator. Track measured margins, routing behavior, and macro validation HR@10. Do not infer ownership, satisfaction, compatibility, or simulator traits.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: **Synthesis.** Three starting insights guide the proposal. (1) SASRec already represents sequential context; a recent-history residual may add redundant information. (2) Users report loose relevance and excessive influence from old, occasional interests; recent, repeated interactions address both, without establishing unmet need or compatibility. (3) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin, motivating discrimination checks rather than implying validation gains.

Three literature insights refine implementation. (1) [MSMT-LSI](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44196-024-00460-5) motivates preference fusion, while [SimRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22136) motivates a competing similarity explanation. (2) [ReZero](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04887) motivates an exactly zero residual coefficient; adding outer LayerNorm would nevertheless change baseline features. (3) [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939) motivates separate negative-count/calibration ablations; [sampled-evaluation research](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) requires selection under the pinned evaluator.

**Framework.** Three directions separate representation, discrimination, and collaborative evidence:

| Direction | Minimal candidate | Diagnostic target |
|---|---|---|
| Temporal intent | Zero-initialized recent residual | Stale interests outweigh current evidence |
| Learning objective | Uniform multi-negative gBCE | Weak lower-tail separation |
| Behavioral relations | Shrunk directed-transition residual | Broad affinity lacks behavioral support |

**Candidate assessment.** Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each dimension includes a benefit and limitation.

- **Recent residual: 3/6/7/6/3.** Originality: targeted adaptation, established fusion. Potential: extensible, possibly redundant. User: targets loose relevance and stale interests, cannot verify compatibility. Model: may improve difficult contexts, no direct margin objective. Difficulty: small module, causal/export consistency needs care.
- **Multi-negative gBCE: 3/7/4/9/4.** Originality: benchmark adaptation, established method. Potential: reusable, calibration-sensitive. User: improves discrimination, lacks current-intent modeling. Model: targets separation, risks false negatives. Difficulty: compact change, increased sampling cost.
- **Transition residual: 4/6/7/6/5.** Originality: support-aware fusion, familiar graph signals. Potential: sparse evidence, limited coverage. User: favors observed connections, cannot prove usability. Model: adds evidence, may amplify popularity. Difficulty: sparse implementation, inference consistency requires work.

**Selected idea: gated recent-history residual.** At 0% progress, retain this feasible architectural experiment, with a bounded scalar initialized exactly to zero. It targets loose historical affinity and old occasional interests; improved Electronics separation remains a hypothesis.

```python
h = sasrec(prefix)
r = layer_norm(normalized_recency_pool(emb(prefix[-k:])))
alpha = 0.5 * tanh(a)  # a initialized exactly to zero
z = h + alpha * r     # preserve existing backbone normalization
scores = z @ candidate_embeddings.T
```

Use causal prefixes and identical predictor scoring. Preserve baseline loss initially; compare untouched baseline, fixed-gate, and learned-gate variants. Check initialization logits and gradients; test outer normalization separately. Last-item, uniform-history, and uniform-recent controls distinguish similarity from recency; timestamp experiments require historical anchors. Preserve routes, seeds, splits, paths, and diagnostics. Predeclare a small, budgeted slate and checkpoint schedule; select only by pinned macro validation HR@10. Report paired category differences and user-bootstrap intervals, acknowledging selection bias. Track margins, gate behavior, popularity, history length, and repeat/nonrepeat slices. Infer neither ownership, satisfaction, compatibility nor simulator traits.
