INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: The best first experiment is **gated recent-and-repeated interest SASRec with multi-negative supervision**. It targets weak adjacent-interest recommendations, excessive influence from older interests, and poor lower-tail score separation. Gains remain hypotheses until measured by pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights motivate the design: (1) SASRec already learns useful ordering, so preserve its collaborative pathway; positive mean margins support this conservative starting point. (2) Electronics has 14.95% negative-over-positive errors and a −0.4812 margin p10, motivating stronger ranking supervision. (3) Users favor recent, repeated interests over loose historical associations, motivating explicit local-context conditioning. These observations do not establish compatibility or unmet need.

Three literature insights organize the alternatives: (1) LSTASRec separates persistent and transient interests [2], suggesting a lightweight gated branch. (2) gSASRec motivates revisiting single-negative supervision [3], while R²NS cautions that immediate hard mining can hurt [4]. (3) TedRec and WhitenRec motivate selective text use but warn that semantic similarity alone lacks discrimination [5,6]; pretrained results do not establish scratch-text gains here.

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Intent | Long/short extractors [2] | Causal recent/repeated pooling | Preferences remain latent |
| Supervision | Generalized BCE [3] | Uniform 16-negative softmax | False negatives remain |
| Content | Text–ID fusion [5,6] | Bounded scratch-text residual | Compatibility unverified |

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are 0–10; difficulty increases with implementation effort. Each entry gives a positive (+) and negative (−) reason.

1. **Gated intent ranking, selected.** Originality 4: + integrates context and ranking; − familiar components. Potential 8: + extensible intent conditioning; − benchmark dependence. User alignment 8: + targets stale and adjacent interests; − cannot verify satisfaction. Model alignment 8: + directly trains competing scores; − margins may not generalize. Difficulty 4: + small SASRec extension; − causal pooling needs care.
2. **Sixteen-negative softmax alone.** Originality 2: + clean hypothesis; − established technique. Potential 6: + reusable baseline; − limited architectural insight. User alignment 4: + stronger discrimination; − no explicit temporal correction. Model alignment 8: + addresses weak margins; − random negatives may be easy. Difficulty 2: + vectorized loss; − extra scoring cost.
3. **Bounded scratch-text residual.** Originality 4: + task-compatible adaptation; − familiar fusion. Potential 7: + content-sensitive matching; − sparse supervision. User alignment 7: + may capture specific formats; − wording cannot certify compatibility. Model alignment 5: + additional discrimination; − may amplify confusion. Difficulty 6: + compact encoder; − tokenization and serving changes.

**Selected algorithm.** At each causal position, gate SASRec features with an exponentially weighted recent-window pool; boost repeated item occurrences using prefix-only counts. Optimize the fused representation against one positive and 16 uniform unseen negatives. This encourages recent/repeated evidence and directly targets weak separation. Ablate pooling, loss, and their combination; select solely by prescribed macro validation HR@10. Preserve all four routes, seeds, predictor parity, and diagnostic contracts.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The best first experiment is **identity-preserving recent-interest SASRec with separately tested multi-negative supervision**. It targets stale interests, loosely adjacent recommendations, and weak lower-tail separation. Improvements remain hypotheses until measured by pinned macro validation HR@10.

**Insights and directions.** Three starting-point insights motivate the design: (1) Positive mean margins support preserving SASRec’s collaborative pathway. (2) Electronics’ 14.95% ordering-error rate and −0.4812 margin p10 motivate stronger discrimination. (3) Users prefer specific recent/repeated interests over broad historical associations, motivating recent-context conditioning. Purchases do not establish satisfaction, compatibility, or unmet need; exact-item recurrence must first be measured.

Three literature insights refine the alternatives: (1) LSTASRec motivates separating transient and persistent interests [2]. (2) gSASRec demonstrates loss–negative-count interactions, while R²NS cautions against immediate hard mining [3,4]. (3) TedRec and WhitenRec motivate selective text use, but pretrained results do not validate scratch-text gains [5,6]. ReZero supplies an exact identity initialization [7]; ComiRec distinguishes latent recurring interests from repeated IDs [8].

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed experiment | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Intent | Long/short extractors [2] | Causal recent residual | Specific fit remains latent |
| Supervision | Generalized BCE [3] | Matched-count loss comparisons | False negatives remain |
| Content | Text–ID fusion [5,6] | Bounded scratch-text residual | Compatibility unverified |
| Recurrence | Multi-interest representations [8] | Training-only recurrence audit | Repeats may be removed |

**Candidate assessments.** Scores are 0–10; higher difficulty means more implementation effort. Each score includes positive (+) and negative (−) reasoning.

1. **Recent residual with staged supervision, selected.** Originality 3: + targeted integration; − established components. Potential 7: + extensible context conditioning; − uncertain transfer. User alignment 7: + targets stale and adjacent interests; − cannot verify unmet need. Model alignment 7: + tests competing-score discrimination; − training margins may mislead. Difficulty 4: + small branch; − parity and controlled sampling require care.
2. **Sixteen-negative candidate cross-entropy alone.** Originality 2: + clean hypothesis; − established. Potential 6: + reusable baseline; − limited architectural insight. User alignment 4: + improved discrimination possible; − no temporal correction. Model alignment 8: + targets weak margins; − negatives may be easy. Difficulty 3: + vectorized; − sampling costs matter.
3. **Bounded scratch-text residual.** Originality 4: + task-compatible adaptation; − familiar fusion. Potential 7: + content-sensitive matching; − sparse supervision. User alignment 7: + possible format cues; − compatibility unproven. Model alignment 5: + additional signal; − possible confusion. Difficulty 6: + compact encoder; − serving/tokenization changes.

**Selected algorithm.** Add `α·LN(project(causal_recent_pool))` to unchanged SASRec features, initializing α=0. Use a five-position window with decay 0.8; remove unconditional repeat boosting because deduplication may eliminate its signal. Separately test 16-negative candidate cross-entropy, then combine validated components. Preserve embedding conventions and verify zero-gate equality, padding handling, and causality. Compare last-item, uniform-window, and exponential pooling under identical branch settings; reserve learned pooling and recurrence weighting for supported follow-ups. Preserve all routes, seeds, predictor parity, diagnostics, and the shared budget. Report category results, search extent, and paired uncertainty; sampled HR@10 does not establish full-catalog quality [9,10].
