{
  "description": "Extend each category’s SASRec with a causal recent-interest residual while preserving the original model exactly at initialization. At position t, pool at most the five most recent nonpadding input embeddings at positions j ≤ t using normalized weights 0.8^(t−j). Let b_t = LayerNorm(project(pool_t)) and h_t = h_SASRec,t + α b_t, where α is a learned scalar initialized to zero. Preserve the backbone, its final normalization, and its scoring embeddings. Test multi-negative supervision separately before combining it with the residual. Exact-item repeat weighting becomes a conditional follow-up rather than a required component.",
  "motivation": "The selected idea explicitly addresses two user-facing failures: older or occasional interests receiving excessive influence, and loosely adjacent items displacing choices supported by current behavior. Recent-context features provide a hypothesis for improving both, although recency alone cannot establish specific functional fit, compatibility, or unmet need. Richer negative supervision addresses the model-facing weak-margin and wrong-ordering failures, especially Electronics’ margin p10 of −0.4812 and 14.95% negative-over-positive rate. Embedding similarity scores of 0.5392–0.5566 are near the mapped zero-cosine baseline of 0.5 and do not support prioritizing collapse regularization. Exact-ID repetition cannot represent repeated interests when preprocessing removes user–item duplicates; latent recurring interests across distinct IDs require separate evidence.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, unchanged input splits, all four category routes, and trial-local artifacts. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS, whose host default is 200. Execute each trial on one GPU and account for all training, diagnostics, debugging, and failed trials within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Use only permitted training interactions, titles, descriptions, and timestamps; the broader metadata examples do not authorize unavailable reviews, ratings, prices, stores, or item categories.\n\nPreserve the actual RecHarness SASRec backbone conventions. The original SASRec implementation scales encoder input embeddings by sqrt(d), leaves scoring embeddings unscaled, and applies final feature normalization; verify and retain the supplied baseline’s corresponding behavior. For the primary residual, pool raw unscaled item embeddings, use a bias-free d-by-d linear projection, and place LayerNorm inside the residual branch with eps=1e-5. Initialize the projection with a recorded initialization rule and initialize α to exactly zero. Initialize new modules without changing the baseline parameter initialization or training-data random streams. A finite-bias sigmoid gate is not an exact identity initializer, and adding LayerNorm after fusion would change the baseline even when α=0. Compare sqrt(d)-scaled pooling only as a separately logged ablation because normalization epsilon and optimization can still affect equivalence.\n\nFor each training position t, include only nonpadding input items at j ≤ t within the five-position window. Define weights as 0.8^(t−j), normalize over valid positions, and return a zero pool for empty windows. Explicitly zero the normalized branch for empty windows so learned LayerNorm offsets cannot introduce padding features. Do not broadcast the final window’s pool across earlier training positions. Keep the baseline attention and padding masks unchanged during the fusion comparison; any backbone mask correction requires a separate experiment. A zero-gate equality check assumes finite branch outputs. Check future-token invariance of earlier valid outputs and padding invariance of the pooling operation; do not require the positional backbone to be invariant to shifts in absolute positions.\n\nBefore any repetition extension, report from permitted training histories the fraction of events beyond each user’s first occurrence of an item and the fraction of next-item training targets already present in their causal input prefix. Record both denominators. These audit results do not establish purchase frequency. If all user–item pairs are unique, omit exact-ID repeat weighting. If recurrence survives, test it separately using count c from the available causal input prefix and multiplier min(2, 1 + log(1 + max(c − 1, 0))); the explicit cap corrects the earlier unbounded formula. Window truncation limits observable prefix counts. Distinct-ID recurrence could later be studied using training-only collaborative clusters, but cluster assignments and any fit decisions must exclude validation/test interactions and must not be described as verified semantic interests.\n\nStage experiments to preserve attribution. First compare unchanged SASRec against the residual using the baseline loss and negative protocol. Next compare losses at a matched negative count of 16 on the unchanged architecture. For a fully specified controlled comparison, draw 16 negatives independently and uniformly with replacement from the category’s nonpadding catalog excluding the current positive. Retain duplicate draws in both losses. Define BCE as softplus(−s_pos) + mean_k softplus(s_neg,k), giving the positive and aggregate negative terms equal weight. Define candidate cross-entropy as −log_softmax([s_pos, s_neg,1, ..., s_neg,16])[0] with temperature 1.0 and no sampling-probability correction. Call this uncorrected candidate cross-entropy, rather than corrected sampled softmax. Share negative draws, initial backbone weights, training examples, minibatch order, optimizer settings, training schedule, and checkpoint-selection opportunities across the matched-count loss comparison. Use a separate sampler RNG so negative generation does not change dropout or data-order streams. Comparing one versus 16 negatives changes supervision count and should be reported separately from the matched-count loss comparison.\n\nExclude padding and the current target in the controlled sampler; treat exclusion of every observed training-history item as a separate sampling ablation because it changes the negative distribution. Never inspect held-out positives to filter negatives. Detect empty eligible catalogs rather than allowing infinite rejection loops. Record realized negative counts, duplicate rates, and sampling exclusions. Uniform sampling does not remove false negatives. Do not import gSASRec calibration claims into a different sampling scheme or objective.\n\nUse a fixed predeclared subset of controls within the remaining budget: unchanged SASRec, last-item residual, uniform five-position residual, and exponential five-position residual. Hold projection, normalization, embedding scale, optimization, and validation opportunities constant. Standalone last-item and exponential-pool dot-product scorers can reveal whether gains mainly reflect local co-occurrence; learned pooling is a later control, not a prerequisite. Test the combined residual and candidate-cross-entropy model only after the component comparisons. Keep the baseline optimizer, batch size, maximum sequence length, and other unspecified settings unchanged and serialize their concrete values. Record every trial configuration, elapsed GPU time, epoch, and selected checkpoint. Keep the baseline validation cadence fixed across compared variants; break exact validation ties by the earlier checkpoint, then by a predeclared configuration order. Do not expand search opportunistically after small gains.\n\nSelect solely using the pinned macro validation HR@10 for 200 users per category and 99 unique negative candidates with evaluation seed 42. Report all four category results. One changed hit moves category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125 percentage points. When authorized evaluator outputs expose per-user hits, compute paired differences and a stratified paired bootstrap interval over the fixed users; otherwise mark paired uncertainty unavailable rather than reconstructing or modifying the evaluator. Such intervals are conditional on the fixed candidate sets and do not correct selection bias from repeated search. Record search extent and reserve held-out evaluation for final confirmation. Full-catalog simulator feedback from twenty real training users remains a separate qualitative assessment and must not be substituted for the selection metric.\n\nSerialize the exact architecture configuration, α, projection, branch normalization, backbone, and scoring weights beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the same model and all four routes. Keep seen-item filtering consistent with the existing evaluation/recommendation interfaces. Test checkpoint round-trip score agreement, zero-gate feature/logit equality in evaluation mode, causal prefix behavior, and empty-window handling.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). view.model must be the actual trained module; view.item_emb must expose the deployed scoring embeddings; view.log2feats must return fused features; and view.predict_score must reproduce deployed candidate scoring. Accept batches (None, sequence, positive, negative) of shape B x L, use only valid positive positions, and return metrics with their measured definitions and denominators. Preserve the original margin and collapse definitions and hold diagnosis users, negative draws, epsilon=0.5, and sampling protocol fixed across variants. Measure collapse on the actual scoring embeddings without treating its mapped value as a calibrated diversity score. Category breakdowns must explicitly be unavailable because item categories are unavailable. The interpretation prompt must use measured metrics and definitions, distinguish training-position diagnostics from validation HR@10, and avoid inventing compatibility, satisfaction, or user-trait measurements.",
  "pseudocode": "freeze experiment list, validation cadence, tie rules, and GPU-budget allocation\nfor category in four_categories:\n    load unchanged permitted split with preprocessing seed 0\n    audit training-only exact-ID recurrence; do not alter preprocessing\n    initialize baseline backbone with training seed 42\n    initialize residual using an isolated RNG; alpha = 0\n    for epoch in configured_epochs:  # host default SASREC_EPOCHS=200\n        for _, sequence, positive, negative in train_loader:\n            base = unchanged_SASRec.log2feats(sequence)\n            recent, nonempty = causal_pool(\n                raw_item_embeddings(sequence), sequence != 0,\n                window=5, decay=0.8)\n            branch = LayerNorm(bias_free_projection(recent))\n            branch = where(nonempty, branch, 0)\n            fused = base + alpha * branch\n            valid = positive != 0\n            if experiment uses baseline supervision:\n                loss = unchanged_baseline_loss(fused, positive, negative, valid)\n            else:\n                candidates = uniform_draws_with_replacement(\n                    count=16, exclude_padding=True,\n                    exclude_current_target=True, separate_rng=True)\n                pos_score = dot(fused, scoring_embedding(positive))\n                neg_scores = dot(fused, scoring_embedding(candidates))\n                if matched_count_BCE:\n                    loss = mean_valid(softplus(-pos_score)\n                                      + mean_neg(softplus(neg_scores)))\n                else:\n                    logits = concat(pos_score, neg_scores) / 1.0\n                    loss = mean_valid(-log_softmax(logits)[..., 0])\n            optimize(loss, unchanged_optimizer_configuration)\n        at fixed baseline validation opportunities:\n            save eligible trial-local checkpoints and measured validation outputs\n    serialize exact architecture and emit matching predictor\nrun predeclared architecture and matched-count loss controls within shared budget\ncombine components only after their separate comparisons\nselect configuration using pinned four-category macro validation HR@10\nreport category results, search extent, uncertainty availability, and diagnostic limits",
