{
  "description": "Add a causal recency-weighted item-embedding residual to SASRec, explicitly bounded relative to each backbone feature’s norm, preserving per-dataset training and routing.",
  "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests recent and repeated interactions may identify specific interests better than broad historical affinity. This remains a hypothesis: SASRec may already encode the signal, and recency or repetition may create shortcuts. Early research progress favors testing this small modification alongside controlled alternatives before proposing a larger architecture.",
  "implementation_notes": "Use only public histories and available titles, descriptions and timestamps; disregard the generic input examples containing unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices or stores. Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four dataset routes, unchanged splits and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment override with default 200. Save configuration, weights and logs beneath each trial directory. For each valid position, pool its last min(20, valid-history-length) embeddings with weights proportional to 2^(-j/5), where j=0 is the latest valid event. Decay is by interaction position, not elapsed time. Use the actual SASRec item embedding table, excluding padding. Set v to the pooled vector, with no additional branch projection, normalization or dropout in the initial experiment. Compute y=h+0.5*sigmoid(a)*||h||₂*v/(||v||₂+1e-8), initializing the trainable scalar a=log(0.1/0.9). Apply the bound after any branch transformations introduced in later controls; do not apply a further feature normalization after addition. Set padded outputs to zero. This construction bounds the added vector relative to h; it does not bound logits or guarantee improved ranking. Keep the baseline scoring function and training loss. Use the same causal computation at every training position and the final valid inference position. Update the predictor writer and checkpoint configuration to reconstruct pooling, gate and scoring exactly. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis); return metrics and metric_definitions. view.model must be the trained module, log2feats must return modified features, and predict_score must follow the trained scoring path. Keep user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility attributes without inventing missing content. Retain embedding and training-margin probes, clearly labeling their limits. Add valid sample counts, missing-value states, final-position pairwise ranks with explicit ties, and residual/backbone norm-ratio quantiles; report zero-backbone positions separately. Mask padded positives and negatives appropriately. Compare baseline, exponential, uniform-last-20 and last-item pooling; all residual variants share the gate and bound. A gate-only control means fixing the trained backbone and item embeddings while learning only a; fixed mixing means fixing sigmoid(a)=0.1. Treat these and ungated addition, parameter-matched backbone projection, unique-item/latest-occurrence pooling and history-edit controls as additional experiments requiring explicit budget allocation. Ungated addition is a diagnostic comparator and lacks the proposed relative bound. Report duplicate prevalence before interpreting frequency controls. Predeclare the checkpoint schedule, promotion criteria, comparison set and deterministic tie rule before execution; absent baseline schedule and timing information, a complete executable search schedule cannot be specified honestly. Use pilot timing within the shared budget to determine feasibility, reserving enough compute to complete finalists. Retain all categories in every promoted comparison, use aggregate GPU-seconds rather than wall time, and record failed runs and diagnostics. Rank completed configurations by pinned macro validation HR@10; use baseline preference then a fixed configuration order for exact ties. Save per-user binary hits when the permitted validation interface exposes them. Use paired category-stratified bootstrap resampling, for example 10,000 draws with seed 42, and report category differences, discordant-user counts and percentile 95% intervals. These intervals condition on fitted models, fixed candidates and the four categories; they exclude training-seed and negative-sampling variation and do not correct adaptive selection. Treat them as exploratory, freeze the winner, and leave final held-out assessment to the host. No training results or full-catalog gains are claimed.",
  "pseudocode": "Predeclare compared configurations, checkpoint policy, tie order and compute reserve.\nFor each configuration and each of the four datasets:\n  initialize baseline SASRec using the required seed policy\n  if residual enabled: initialize a = log(0.1 / 0.9)\n  for each training batch (None, sequence, positive, negative):\n    h = backbone.log2feats(sequence)\n    for each valid position t:\n      ids = last <=20 nonpadding events at positions <=t\n      if exponential: weights[j] = 2**(-j/5), newest j=0\n      if uniform: weights[j] = 1\n      if last_item: retain only newest event\n      v[t] = sum(weights * item_emb(ids)) / sum(weights)\n    r = 0.5 * sigmoid(a) * norm(h) * v / (norm(v) + 1e-8)\n    features = mask_padding(h + r)\n    loss = unchanged_baseline_loss(features, positive, negative)\n    update trainable parameters\n    account for GPU time and stop according to the declared budget policy\n  at declared checkpoints:\n    evaluate using unchanged pinned validation protocol\n    log category HR@10, available per-user hits, diagnostics and compute\nCompare configurations using the four-category macro HR@10 only.\nUse paired bootstrap intervals descriptively; apply the declared exact-tie rule.\nFreeze the winner and write predictor code loading its exact configuration and weights.\nDo not inspect hidden test files or tune after the winner is frozen.",
