INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for diagnosis in iteration 1:
{
  "description": "Add a learned recency-weighted item-embedding residual inside each dataset’s SASRec sequence-feature function. Preserve all four independently routed models, shared dot-product scoring, and selection by pinned macro validation HR@10. Treat this as an empirical adaptation of established historical-similarity methods rather than a new model class.",
  "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests that broad historical affinity sometimes outranks specific recent or repeated interests. A small historical-evidence branch tests this hypothesis without unavailable ratings, reviews, ownership labels, simulator traits, or pretrained weights. FISM, FPMC, and FOSSIL substantially limit originality claims. Uniform-history, last-item, and popularity controls are needed before attributing improvements specifically to recency.",
  "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, unchanged benchmark splits, all four categories and routing, single-GPU execution, and artifacts beneath the trial directory. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS=200 unless explicitly configured otherwise within the authorized search. Charge training, gate fitting, diagnostics, debugging, and failed trials to the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. No improvement has been measured.\n\nUse a fixed four-interaction half-life as a declared hypothesis, not an established optimum. Set lambda=2**(-0.25). For each retained chronological sequence window, initialize FP32 S=0 and Z=0. At each nonpadding item x_t, update S=lambda*S+E(x_t), Z=lambda*Z+1, and emit p_t=S/max(Z,epsilon). Padding leaves S and Z unchanged and emits zero. Empty prefixes return zero. Reset at every retained window boundary in training and inference; do not carry discarded history. The pool includes sequence[t], which precedes positive[t], and never includes positive[t] or subsequent interactions. Repeated occurrences contribute separately. This pooling measures interaction recency, not elapsed timestamp recency.\n\nDefine shared features f_t=h_t+sigmoid(a)*p_t and logits f_t dot E(candidate). Learn one scalar a per routed dataset, initialized to logit(0.1). Use no projection initially, preserving the atomic intervention. Keep pooling differentiable during joint training, mask padded targets, and retain the baseline loss and other settings. The bounded gate does not bound the residual’s contribution relative to SASRec because embedding and feature norms differ; report gate values, residual/base-logit magnitudes, and item-norm versus training-frequency associations. A cosine-residual diagnostic can test norm dependence, but is not part of the primary architecture.\n\nImplement the residual in the actual nn.Module’s shared log2feats path so view.log2feats returns augmented features and view.predict_score uses the existing dot product. Never add the residual twice. In inference select the last valid position, returning a zero representation for an empty sequence consistently with the documented baseline fallback. Save gate, half-life, architecture version, and all parameters. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the identical architecture and routing. Verify recurrence against explicit normalized decay weights, current-item/next-target alignment, padding invariance, empty inputs, truncated windows, and training/inference logits with dropout disabled.\n\nBefore execution, record the number of gate-fitting runs, joint-training runs, checkpoint evaluations, checkpoint schedule, and GPU-second allocations; apply the same checkpoint rules to baseline and joint-training residual. A minimal screen uses one frozen SASRec checkpoint per dataset and fits only scalar gates for normalized uniform, recency, last-item, and log-training-frequency branches, alongside the unchanged zero-residual baseline. Fit gates with training batches, never validation labels. Define the popularity branch as sigmoid(a_pop)*log1p(c_i), where c_i counts only training interactions. Keep windows, candidate embeddings, loss, and initialization fixed for the three embedding-pool controls. Retrain the predeclared recency model and compare it with the baseline under matched conditions; do not expand the search in response to small validation gains without recording the additional queries. Frozen comparisons isolate score-combination effects but cannot establish behavior after joint retraining. Attribute a benefit specifically to recency only if evidence also supports superiority over the simpler controls.\n\nSelect solely through host-provided pinned macro validation HR@10 with 200 users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Never replace the evaluator or change its candidate sets. Log every evaluated checkpoint. Report four category deltas and their equal-weight macro 